  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight...A Flood Watch continues across all of east \ncentral Florida through late this evening. Daytime heating near to \nsouth of a stalled front across the area has increased instability, \nwith mesoscale analysis showing MLCAPE up to 1000-1500 J/kg across \nmuch of the region. Showers and storms across SW Florida are moving \nslowly northeast and should increase in coverage, especially with \nboundary interactions with the inland moving east coast sea breeze \nacross the southern interior. Numerous showers and storms should \nresult and also push slowly northeast and offshore into this \nevening. Deep moisture, with record PW values for the day around 2.4-\n2.5\" and slower motion of developing convection will lead to quick \nrainfall accumulations of 2-4\", with a low potential for localized\ntotals of 5-7\". This may lead to flash flooding in some spots, \nespecially across those areas where heavy rainfall has already \noccurred recently. A Slight Risk (Level 2 of 4) for excessive \nrainfall exists across much of the area. \n\nConvection should diminish or shift offshore into late evening, with \nsome lingering rain chances (up to 20-40%) overnight, mainly north \nand east of Orlando as an onshore flow persists north of the front. \nHumid conditions will persist, with lows tonight in the low to \nmid 70s. \n  \nFriday-Sunday...Hurricane Isaias will lift northward across the \ncentral Gulf, making landfall across the northern Gulf coast into \nFriday night. This will shift the stalled front across the area\ngradually northward through late week and into the weekend, with\nE/SE flow Friday becoming more gusty into Saturday as it becomes\nS/SE. While overall PW values do decrease a tad as front lifts\nnorth, they remain anomalously high (around 2.0-2.3\"). This will \ncontinue scattered to numerous showers and storms each day from \nlate week through the weekend, with greatest coverage during the \nafternoon and evening hours. Highest rain chances will be near to \nnorth of Orlando on Friday (around 60-80%) and drop to 30-50% \nacross central and southern portions of the area tomorrow. As \ndeeper moisture continues to build in the southerly flow, PoPs \nrise to 80% Saturday and then fall slightly to 50-70% on Sunday.\nA few storms could be strong each day, producing wind gusts near \n45 mph. An increase in storm motion and initial drop in PW values \non Friday should lower the risk for locally heavy rainfall, but as\nmoisture again rises into Saturday, a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of \n4) for excessive rainfall returns across the area. \n\nAs front lifts north and southerly flow increases, temperatures \nwill rise into the upper 80s/near 90 degrees for some spots\nthrough the weekend. Humid conditions will lead to peak heat index\nvalues in the 100-105F range each afternoon. Warm and muggy\nconditions continue overnight, with lows in the 70s. \n\nMonday-Thursday...(Modified Previous Discussion) The trough \ncontaining the remnants of Isaias moves off of the eastern U.S. \ncoast early next week. Meanwhile, high pressure strengthens and \nstacks vertically over the northern Gulf Coast, moving eastward \nthrough the period. A weak front associated with the trough will \npush southward across the area Monday, which will finally lower\nrain chances across the area and lead to less humid conditions. \nPoPs lower to 20-30% Monday as front moves through, with dry\nconditions then forecast for Tuesday through Thursday. Northerly \nflow prevails through mid-week, as the ridge moves eastward, \nveering onshore late in the period. High temperatures overall \nremain in the mid to upper 80s. However, much less humid \nconditions Tuesday and Wednesday will limit heat indices to the \nlower to mid-90s. Overnight lows also dip into the 60s.",
